column5:
Alex Honnold, a rock climber who was the subject of the Oscar-winning documentary {LQ}Free Solo,{RQ} successfully climbed the 1,667-foot-tall skyscraper Taipei 101 in Taiwan on Sunday, without a rope. Scaling a building that tall is not like climbing a mountain.

Those in the club of just a dozen or so known skyscraper climbers worldwide say the activity comes with unique physical and mental demands. It is also a largely underground sport because it is usually illegal. Alain Robert, a Frenchman who has scaled some 200 buildings since the 1990s, mostly with his bare hands, said that he has been arrested more than 170 times. {LQ}You feel like you're literally in a movie,{RQ} Mr. Robert said, with {LQ}cops that are trying to catch the bad guy climbing the building.{RQ}

The obvious risks make it rare for someone to obtain permission to climb a tall building, as Mr. Honnold did. His climb on Sunday was broadcast live on Netflix. {LQ}I've never been willing to get arrested,{RQ} the 40-year-old said in a podcast recorded before the climb. Unlike the serene natural landscape surrounding climbers on routes like El Capitan in Yosemite National Park, which Mr. Honnold scaled in 2017, skyscraper climbers must overcome urban noise, crowds and, occasionally, police officers determined to arrest them.

